{
 "id": "2019-serranomontes-livestock",
 "name": "Livestock and Landscape Preference",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the image-based questionnaire of Serrano-Montes, Martínez-Ibarra & Arias-García (2019). Does livestock make a rural landscape more beautiful? Participants see five pairs of photographs of the Spanish dehesa (oak wood pasture), taken by the authors on a single day in May 2016 near Cardeña, Córdoba, and choose the landscape they find more beautiful in each pair, judging the landscape rather than the photograph. The pairs are: A, cattle vs. a similar scene without cattle; B, horses vs. water; C, cattle vs. a traditional farmhouse; D, pigs vs. a similar scene without pigs; E, cattle vs. denser, more diverse vegetation. Gender, age, education, job and whether the respondent is a livestock farmer are asked at the end. Analysis as in the paper: the percentage choosing the scene with animals in each pair, overall and by group (village residents vs. city residents, men vs. women, livestock farmers). Of 520 face-to-face respondents in Cardeña and Granada, the scene with animals was chosen by 76.7% in pair A, 32.3% in B, 43.5% in C, 71.9% in D and 61.9% in E. Source: Serrano-Montes, J. L., Martínez-Ibarra, E. & Arias-García, J. (2019), How Does the Presence of Livestock Influence Landscape Preferences? An Image-Based Approach, Landscape Online 71: 1-18, doi:10.3097/LO.201971, CC BY 4.0. Photographs are cropped from its Figure 2. The template will be removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper printed the pairs in colour on A3 paper and interviewed people face to face in Cardeña and Granada; here the pairs are shown on screen and anyone can take part; (2) the paper's questionnaire also had four pairs without animals as distractors; these are not printed in the paper and are left out; (3) the photographs are the copies printed in Figure 2, about 420 px wide; (4) the paper only says that gender, age, education level and job were asked, so the wording and answer options are ours; we add a yes/no question on whether the respondent is a livestock farmer because the paper analysed farmers separately; (5) the instruction wording is ours, following the paper's note that respondents judged the beauty of the landscape, not the photograph.",
